.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt IA32 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ia32
.Nd 32-bit code and i386 system calls under the x86-64 kernel
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The x86-64 kernel runs i386 programs, ELF32 executables for i386 with
ELF32 interpreters, in 32-bit mode, in the layout and with the initial
stack and auxiliary vector that
.Xr execve 2
describes, and maps them the 32-bit library of
.Xr vdso 7 .
Such a program starts with CS 0x23 and DS, ES and SS 0x2b, and its
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Fn mmap2 ,
.Xr mremap 2 ,
.Xr munmap 2
and
.Xr mprotect 2
keep below 4 GiB.
.Xr clone 2
takes the i386 order of arguments, and its
.Dv CLONE_SETTLS
argument is a
.Vt struct user_desc
whose descriptor the child gets, as
.Xr set_thread_area 2
sets one.
A signal that dumps core leaves an ELF32 core file of i386's notes, as
.Xr core 5
describes.
.Pp
The kernel lays out its global descriptor table (GDT) as Linux
does, so that a 64-bit process can run 32-bit code and make i386 system
calls, as a Linux with its 32-bit emulation lets it:
.Bl -column "Selector" "Entries" -offset indent
.It Sy Selector Ta Sy Entries Ta Sy Segment
.It 0x10 Ta 2 Ta kernel code
.It 0x18 Ta 3 Ta kernel data
.It 0x23 Ta 4 Ta user 32-bit code
.It 0x2b Ta 5 Ta user data, the stack segment of both modes
.It 0x33 Ta 6 Ta user 64-bit code
.It 0x40 Ta 8, 9 Ta the processor's TSS
.It 0x50 Ta 10, 11 Ta the address space's LDT, as
.Xr modify_ldt 2
describes it
.It 0x63, 0x6b, 0x73 Ta 12 to 14 Ta the thread's TLS entries
.El
.Pp
A far return or far jump to selector 0x23 runs the code it names in
32-bit mode, and one to 0x33 brings it back to 64-bit mode.
The 32-bit code's addresses, its stack among them, must lie below 4 GiB.
.Ss System calls
.Ql int $0x80
is an i386 system call, from 32-bit code or from 64-bit code: the number
in EAX is the i386 number, and the arguments are EBX, ECX, EDX, ESI, EDI
and EBP, each zero-extended to 64 bits.
The kernel runs the call at that number in its compat table of i386
calls and returns the result in EAX.
A call interrupted by a signal is restarted by executing the
.Ql int $0x80
again, as on i686.
.Pp
.Fn __kernel_vsyscall
of the i386 library of
.Xr vdso 7
enters by the faster instruction the processor takes from 32-bit code, as
Linux's does: by
.Ql sysenter
on Intel's, Centaur's and Zhaoxin's processors, and by
.Ql syscall
on AMD's and Hygon's.
The kernel sets it up at boot, after the copy of the library's text,
keeping
.Ql int $0x80
on a processor it does not know.
Either entry makes the call the
.Ql int $0x80
that follows it in the library would make, with the same registers, and
the kernel's frame of the call names the address after that instruction,
so that a tracer, a signal frame and a restart see the call as made by
.Ql int $0x80 ;
a restart executes that instruction.
The kernel returns by
.Ql sysret
when the frame still names that address in 32-bit code with the flat
segments, and by
.Ql iret
otherwise.
.Pp
The table has a row for each of Linux 6.12's i386 numbers, 452 of them.
233 rows run the x86-64 handler of a call whose arguments and structures
have one layout in both interfaces, such as
.Xr getpid 2 ,
.Xr kill 2
and
.Xr close 2 ,
or whose handler reads the i386 layout itself, such as
.Xr readv 2 ,
.Xr adjtimex 2
and the socket calls, whose message headers, control messages aligned
to 4 bytes,
.Xr recvmmsg 2
timeout and time-limit options are the i386 ones
.Pq Xr send 2 , Xr recv 2 , Xr setsockopt 2 ,
the System V IPC and message queue calls, whose records without
.Dv IPC_64 ,
with it and of a message are the i386 ones
.Pq Xr ipc 2 , Xr mq_open 2 ,
.Xr futex 2
with its 32-bit robust list, and the calls that write a resource usage
or a CPU mask;
118 run a compat handler, which turns the i386 structures and the 64-bit
arguments i386 splits over two registers, low word first, into the x86-64
handler's and its results back, as Linux's compat calls do, or, for
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Fn execveat ,
.Xr clone 2 ,
the old
.Xr mmap 2
and
.Fn mmap2 ,
does the i386 call's own work;
12 run a compat handler that returns
.Er ENOSYS
until its translation is written; 89 are empty, where Linux has no call
or this kernel has no handler, and return
.Er ENOSYS ,
as does a number with no row.
.Pp
The translated calls are the stat and statfs families, the calls with
split offsets, lengths and advice such as
.Nm pread64 ,
.Nm ftruncate64 ,
.Nm fallocate
and
.Nm sync_file_range ,
.Nm getdents ,
.Nm readdir
and the locks of
.Nm fcntl64 ,
the vectored reads and writes,
.Nm select
and
.Nm poll
in their forms, the time calls with both the 32-bit and the 64-bit
timespec,
.Xr socketcall 2 ,
whose arguments are 32-bit words, the calls of 16-bit user and group IDs,
.Nm nice ,
.Nm umount ,
the old
.Xr uname 2
forms, the limits of
.Xr prlimit64 2 Ns 's
older calls,
.Nm sysinfo ,
.Nm times
and
.Nm sched_rr_get_interval .
A value too wide for its i386 field is
.Er EOVERFLOW
where Linux's compat call answers so.
A sleep,
.Nm select
or
.Nm poll
that a handled signal interrupts returns
.Er EINTR ,
even under
.Dv SA_RESTART ,
as its x86-64 form and Linux's do.
.Xr set_thread_area 2
and
.Xr get_thread_area 2
are the i386 calls' own.
.Pa rach2/syscalls.md
lists the calls the table runs in its compat column.
.Pp
An i386
.Xr ioctl 2
is taken by the type of its command, the second byte of its number, as
Linux's
.Fn compat_ioctl
handlers take it.
A command whose argument has one layout in both interfaces runs as the
x86-64 call; one whose structure differs, such as
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_VERSION ,
.Dv SIOCGIFCONF
or
.Dv LOOP_GET_STATUS ,
is translated to the x86-64 structure and its result back to the i386
one; a command of a type no family takes, or one Linux refuses from
i386 code, returns
.Er ENOTTY .
A read of an evdev node gives an i386 caller records of 16 bytes, with
32-bit seconds and microseconds.
.Pa rach2/ia32.md
lists the families and what each translates.
.Pp
For a call that entered by
.Ql int $0x80 ,
a
.Xr seccomp 2
filter sees
.Dv AUDIT_ARCH_I386
and the i386 number, and the strict mode allows the i386 numbers of
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr write 2 ,
.Xr exit 2
and
.Xr sigreturn 2 ;
.Xr ptrace 2 Ns 's
.Dv PTRACE_GET_SYSCALL_INFO
reports
.Dv AUDIT_ARCH_I386 ,
the i386 number, and the result taken in 32 bits.
.Pp
A
.Ql syscall
or
.Ql sysenter
made by 32-bit code outside the library also makes the i386 call in EAX,
and returns to the library's return path, as on Linux, which a program
must not rely on.
.Ss Thread-local storage
A thread has three TLS descriptors, entries 12 to 14, which
.Xr set_thread_area 2
sets and
.Xr get_thread_area 2
reads by
.Ql int $0x80 .
The kernel writes them into the GDT on every switch to the thread, a
child made by
.Xr fork 2
or
.Xr clone 2
starts with its parent's, and
.Xr execve 2
empties them.
A data segment register that holds a TLS entry's selector is loaded
again on every switch to the thread, which sets its base from the
descriptor.
A tracer's
.Dv PTRACE_POKEUSER
or
.Dv PTRACE_SETREGS
may write into a data segment register the null selector, 3, 0x23, 0x2b,
0x33, the selector of a TLS entry the tracee has set or any selector of
the LDT at privilege 3, and into CS 0x23, 0x33 or a selector of the LDT; a
data segment register whose selector does not load gets the null selector
at the switch, and a CS that does not load is the tracee's fault, as on
Linux.
.Ss Signals
A handler installed by the x86-64
.Xr sigaction 2
runs in 64-bit mode with an x86-64 frame, also for a signal taken in
32-bit code.
The frame saves CS and SS as they were; its
.Va uc_flags
has
.Dv UC_SIGCONTEXT_SS ,
and
.Dv UC_STRICT_RESTORE_SS
only for a signal taken in 64-bit code.
.Nm rt_sigreturn
restores CS when it is 0x33, or 0x23 or a present code segment of
privilege 3 in the LDT with an instruction pointer below 4 GiB, and SS when it is a present, writable data segment of privilege 3,
expand-up or expand-down;
a CS it cannot restore, or an SS it cannot restore with
.Dv UC_STRICT_RESTORE_SS
set or with a CS of 32-bit code, ends the process with
.Dv SIGSEGV ,
as Linux's return does.
Any other SS it cannot restore becomes 0x2b, as Linux makes it for a
64-bit program that saved no SS.
.Pp
A handler installed by an i386 call,
.Nm signal ,
.Nm sigaction
or
.Nm rt_sigaction
made by
.Ql int $0x80 ,
gets i386's frames instead, from 64-bit code or 32-bit code alike, and
runs in 32-bit code with CS 0x23 and SS 0x2b; the i386 return calls
restore them.
The i386 signal calls take i386's
.Vt sigaction ,
.Vt stack_t
and
.Vt siginfo_t ,
as
.Xr sigaction 2
describes, and a tracer that makes
.Xr ptrace 2
by
.Ql int $0x80
gets i386's words, siginfo and registers.
The register sets of a tracee running 32-bit code are i386's for any
tracer.
.Sh LIMITS
An i386 frame without
.Dv SA_RESTORER
returns through the return code in the frame.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/wow64probe.c
enters 32-bit code, makes i386 calls from both modes, takes a TLS entry
and reads through its selector, takes a signal in 32-bit code and returns
there, takes one with an i386 handler on an alternate stack,
kills a child with a seccomp filter of the i386 getpid, and reads
each call's architecture with
.Dv PTRACE_GET_SYSCALL_INFO .
The i686 probe
.Pa tests/ldtprobe.c ,
which no suite yet runs on this kernel, writes and reads entries of the
LDT, loads and calls through them across a switch, a
.Xr fork 2
and a signal, and makes its calls through
.Fn __kernel_vsyscall .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr arch_prctl 2 ,
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr modify_ldt 2 ,
.Xr ptrace 2 ,
.Xr seccomp 2 ,
.Xr set_thread_area 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2 ,
.Xr syscalls 2 ,
.Xr vdso 7
